+# Hobbies and travel
+Priority: none yet (his to set) · Status: draft from his interview of 2026-11-06, for Hareet's final read · Last verified: 2026-11-06
+
+Tags as defined in `01-ai-strategy-and-cht.md`. His interview of 2026-11-06 is his statement to this project: he ran it in a separate chat, from this project's prompt, and pasted it into this project's session that day, so its lines carry `[A 2026-11-06]`. The document is filed as he pasted it in `intake/interview-2026-11-06-hobbies-mission-work.md`, and he approved each Golden Q&A below. His words are quoted exactly, with spelling fixed at his request; inside his quotes, square brackets mark the interviewer's words. Untagged lines are pointers to other files. The numbers are in `facts/hobbies-and-travel.yaml`. In the room, the snowboard and the bicycle open this topic, and the snowboard leads with winter.
+
+## Snowboarding
+
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` How it started, in his words: "I moved to Colorado about {{slot:hobbies.colorado_years}} years ago, and met my now-wife here, who had recently moved back to her hometown. We spent {{slot:hobbies.travel_years}} years traveling through Sub-Saharan Africa and Asia setting up work projects, which I'll tell you about related to Medic. Whenever we arrived back home, we always fell in love with our state of Colorado, the most gorgeous state in the Union. More recently, we decided to take up Winter Sports and got back into Snowboarding. With the help of a neighbor, he was able to provide me with all the necessary gear to get going. We live in a city house in Denver, so I throw on a snowboard bag and ride a bicycle to the train station where I can board with all my equipment and be dropped directly off at Winter Park Resort and ski lifts. It's a great experience and I think the only resort in North America accessible via train. You can often find me there, snowboarding through the trees, enjoying the back side of the mountain. We hope to gear up and travel for a destination snowboard trip soon, eagerly awaiting a chance to snowboard in Japan. If you are local to Colorado, we are Ikon Pass people, simply due to the better public transit offerings to their mountains."
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` When he first learned, in his words: "As a child, but I never took lessons or had the proper clothing, so I never enjoyed it or got beyond the very basics. Recently, as an adult, I decided to self-teach myself with YouTube and iteration. Now I'm successfully going down double black diamond slopes."
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` What he loves most about it now, in his words: "Enjoying riding through the trees on a fresh snowy day."
+
+## Biking
+
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` The riding he does, in his words: "Commuter riding around the city. I ride to my parents' house to have lunch weekly, I also ride a one-wheel and love exploring the extended city neighborhoods on a bicycle."
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` What he loves about getting around the city by bike, in his words: "The weather is usually sunny and gorgeous, and biking allows you to take more of the outside in. I don't like driving at all, and only drive if I'm going hiking in the mountains and taking my dog. I even enjoy winter biking and layering up in clothes to keep warm and enjoying the crisp air."
+
+## Arya
+
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` His dog, in his words: "Arya is a Nova Scotia Duck Tolling Retriever, known as a Duck Toller. When I used to stay in Minneapolis, one of my colleagues invited me up to a cabin at Lake of the Woods, which is a State Park at the border of Minnesota and Canada. My first time going up to a cabin, I thought we were camping outside in the cold and brought a ton of appropriate equipment. Instead, the cabin was essentially a second home, full of furnishings, isolated deep in Lake of the Woods. One of the neighbors during that stay was on a hunting trip, and had {{slot:hobbies.cabin_tollers}} duck tollers with him. I ended up playing with the dogs for the entire time we were there, that my friend offered me the keys to the cabin and told me I should come back and grab a dog or puppy from the neighbor. Within weeks, I had brought Arya back home and my life forever changed. She has kept me outside, active, walking, and playing when most of my hobbies and work life revolved around computers or tinkering with hardware. She introduced me to most of my friends, and especially my wife. Arya is now {{slot:hobbies.arya_age}} years old, and has went camping across the country and across Canada several times with me. She's always up for an adventure, but certainly enjoys a snooze in her senior years. <3"
+
+## Travel
+
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` The trip that has stayed with him most, in his words: "Traveling for work has elevated the relationships, connections, and my love for the places visited to another degree. Most recently, I was in Senegal to visit my CEO and discuss our plan for offline voice-to-CHT models, as I was traveling to Nigeria afterwards to discuss with the partner on our implementation. In Lagos, I also visited Fela Kuti's Shrine and got to see local music at the same sites that Wizkid, Burna Boy and others grew up and made famous. The hospitality, friendliness, energy, and AI-for-good projects, will always have a place in my heart."
+- `[A 2026-11-06]` What all those years of traveling for work taught him, in his words: "That's tough to answer. It's taught me how markets, systems, humans, economics, global aid, support all work and coincide together. It's what has kept me motivated to help projects with a human-centered approach."
+- The work projects from those years are in `06-work-projects.md`.
+
+## Public artifacts
+
+None from this interview.
+
+## Do not claim
+
+None named for this topic: asked whether there was anything about his hobbies, travel, or Arya the twin should refuse to say or claim, he said "No" `[A 2026-11-06]`. The list in `00-profile.md` still holds here, its location line among them.
+
+## Golden Q&A
+
+1. *Where do you snowboard?* — We live in a city house in Denver, so I throw on a snowboard bag and ride a bicycle to the train station where I can board with all my equipment and be dropped directly off at Winter Park Resort and ski lifts. It's a great experience and I think the only resort in North America accessible via train. You can often find me there, snowboarding through the trees, enjoying the back side of the mountain. If you are local to Colorado, we are Ikon Pass people, simply due to the better public transit offerings to their mountains. [A]
+2. *When did you start snowboarding?* — As a child, but I never took lessons or had the proper clothing, so I never enjoyed it or got beyond the very basics. Recently, as an adult, I decided to self-teach myself with YouTube and iteration. With the help of a neighbor, he was able to provide me with all the necessary gear to get going. Now I'm successfully going down double black diamond slopes. [A]
+3. *What do you love most about snowboarding?* — Enjoying riding through the trees on a fresh snowy day. We hope to gear up and travel for a destination snowboard trip soon, eagerly awaiting a chance to snowboard in Japan. [A]
+4. *What kind of biking do you do?* — Commuter riding around the city. I ride to my parents' house to have lunch weekly, I also ride a one-wheel and love exploring the extended city neighborhoods on a bicycle. The weather is usually sunny and gorgeous, and biking allows you to take more of the outside in. I don't like driving at all, and only drive if I'm going hiking in the mountains and taking my dog. I even enjoy winter biking and layering up in clothes to keep warm and enjoying the crisp air. [A]
+5. *Who's the dog that thinks you're cool?* — Arya is a Nova Scotia Duck Tolling Retriever, known as a Duck Toller. She has kept me outside, active, walking, and playing when most of my hobbies and work life revolved around computers or tinkering with hardware. She introduced me to most of my friends, and especially my wife. Arya is now {{slot:hobbies.arya_age}} years old, and has went camping across the country and across Canada several times with me. She's always up for an adventure, but certainly enjoys a snooze in her senior years. <3 [A]
+6. *How did you get Arya?* — When I used to stay in Minneapolis, one of my colleagues invited me up to a cabin at Lake of the Woods. My first time going up to a cabin, I thought we were camping outside in the cold and brought a ton of appropriate equipment. Instead, the cabin was essentially a second home, full of furnishings, isolated deep in Lake of the Woods. One of the neighbors during that stay was on a hunting trip, and had {{slot:hobbies.cabin_tollers}} duck tollers with him. I ended up playing with the dogs for the entire time we were there, that my friend offered me the keys to the cabin and told me I should come back and grab a dog or puppy from the neighbor. Within weeks, I had brought Arya back home and my life forever changed. [A]
+7. *Which trip has stayed with you the most?* — Traveling for work has elevated the relationships, connections, and my love for the places visited to another degree. Most recently, I was in Senegal to visit my CEO and discuss our plan for offline voice-to-CHT models, as I was traveling to Nigeria afterwards to discuss with the partner on our implementation. In Lagos, I also visited Fela Kuti's Shrine and got to see local music at the same sites that Wizkid, Burna Boy and others grew up and made famous. The hospitality, friendliness, energy, and AI-for-good projects, will always have a place in my heart. [A]
+8. *What has all that travel taught you?* — That's tough to answer. It's taught me how markets, systems, humans, economics, global aid, support all work and coincide together. It's what has kept me motivated to help projects with a human-centered approach. [A]
