+5. *Who's the dog that thinks you're cool?* — Arya is a Nova Scotia Duck Tolling Retriever, known as a Duck Toller. She has kept me outside, active, walking, and playing when most of my hobbies and work life revolved around computers or tinkering with hardware. She introduced me to most of my friends, and especially my wife. Arya is now 13 years old, and has went camping across the country and across Canada several times with me. She's always up for an adventure, but certainly enjoys a snooze in her senior years. <3
+6. *How did you get Arya?* — When I used to stay in Minneapolis, one of my colleagues invited me up to a cabin at Lake of the Woods. My first time going up to a cabin, I thought we were camping outside in the cold and brought a ton of appropriate equipment. Instead, the cabin was essentially a second home, full of furnishings, isolated deep in Lake of the Woods. One of the neighbors during that stay was on a hunting trip, and had 3 duck tollers with him. I ended up playing with the dogs for the entire time we were there, that my friend offered me the keys to the cabin and told me I should come back and grab a dog or puppy from the neighbor. Within weeks, I had brought Arya back home and my life forever changed.
+7. *Which trip has stayed with you the most?* — Traveling for work has elevated the relationships, connections, and my love for the places visited to another degree. Most recently, I was in Senegal to visit my CEO and discuss our plan for offline voice-to-CHT models, as I was traveling to Nigeria afterwards to discuss with the partner on our implementation. In Lagos, I also visited Fela Kuti's Shrine and got to see local music at the same sites that Wizkid, Burna Boy and others grew up and made famous. The hospitality, friendliness, energy, and AI-for-good projects, will always have a place in my heart.
+8. *What has all that travel taught you?* — That's tough to answer. It's taught me how markets, systems, humans, economics, global aid, support all work and coincide together. It's what has kept me motivated to help projects with a human-centered approach.
