+## 05 Mission
+### His words
+- Q: What do you want your work to do for people?
+  A: "In a world where we can build much faster, we need to think harder about what we choose to build and to ensure that we build it safely, securely and focused on the direct user impact. Combining some thoughts around what traveling has taught me, related to my non-profit projects is that we must also focus our AI Engineering tools and skills on supporting the end user. I put this under the umbrella of the phrase of "Runtime AI" and focused on voice-for-CHT and fine-tuning local language models to handle dialects and technical constraints like compression needed to work in low-resourced areas. Being able to identify the cost-effectiveness, the viability, value of new technology for new users is fundamental to my mission. Imagine sharing that focus with a like-minded group of energetic builders."
+  *[Interviewer's note: Hareet said the chalkboard now lists "denverlocal.ai" and links to the org website. A paragraph describing that website will come from his resume, so no questions about it were asked here.]*
+- *[Interviewer's note: Asked what "Runtime AI" means to him, Hareet asked for a cross-reference instead of an answer: Runtime AI is explained in detail elsewhere in the knowledge corpus.]*
+- Q: The corpus describes a lifelong mission to bring the cultural tradition of Seva — selfless service to humanity — into daily work. What does that look like on an ordinary day?
+  A: "It means to show up every day with trust in your fellow peers, and the energy to take that day on as if every day is Day 1 with the importance of knowing what Day 100 looks like. We owe it to our fellow team, to strive in our daily self. Similar to work I do for Medic, finding a team mission and showing up for those around you who share that goal is unifying experience for me."
+- Q: How do you decide whether a new technology is worth bringing to new users?
+  A: "Taking into consideration the effectiveness of using deterministic approaches, and how cost effective the evaluation, resource-usage, and maintenance is on AI solutions. For example, at Medic, our Community Health Workers, had older Android phones with roughly 2-4GB [of RAM] on them, and were mainly without network access. Building the application to be reliant on larger models that were unable to run on their phone was effectively a non-starter. I spent time architecting AI solutions that could run on their phones, which we labeled as Runtime AI in our Medic AI strategy that I co-authored with our CEO. This included fine-tuning offline local language voice models to fill out CHT forms. I was also the winner of AWS Imagine 2025 Momentum to Modernize Award, where I implemented Smart Task Prioritization inside the CHT. This was architected in a manner that once a week, if network access was available, the client would synchronize tasks with a deeper reasoning and higher context model, and on the local offline device it ran a small ML on an existing priority score configuration. A different focus around AI in our strategy was Buildtime AI, and my focus was to pull engineering dreams off the shelf, that were once unattainable or too time consuming, and detail out the implementation path towards them. For Medic, this meant allowing non-technical users the ability to participate in the creation of new features and maintainability of the software. I architected and built the cht-agent, a multi-agent you can deploy, install with all the considerations an SRE brings: cost-effectiveness, model agnostic, locally-owned, context management, curated domain knowledge-corpus."
+  *[Interviewer's note: His next answer corrects the sentence beginning "I was also the winner of AWS Imagine 2025 Momentum to Modernize Award". Medic won the award. He was the author and creator of the project, he designed and architected Smart Task Prioritization, and he leads the community squad building it.]*
