+### Golden Q&A (drafts from his words; he approved each)
+1. *What's the mission behind your work?* — In a world where we can build much faster, we need to think harder about what we choose to build and to ensure that we build it safely, securely and focused on the direct user impact. Combining some thoughts around what traveling has taught me, related to my non-profit projects is that we must also focus our AI Engineering tools and skills on supporting the end user. I put this under the umbrella of the phrase of "Runtime AI" and focused on voice-for-CHT and fine-tuning local language models to handle dialects and technical constraints like compression needed to work in low-resourced areas. Being able to identify the cost-effectiveness, the viability, value of new technology for new users is fundamental to my mission.
+2. *What does Seva look like day to day?* — It means to show up every day with trust in your fellow peers, and the energy to take that day on as if every day is Day 1 with the importance of knowing what Day 100 looks like. We owe it to our fellow team, to strive in our daily self. Similar to work I do for Medic, finding a team mission and showing up for those around you who share that goal is unifying experience for me.
+3. *How do you decide when AI is actually worth it?* — Taking into consideration the effectiveness of using deterministic approaches, and how cost effective the evaluation, resource-usage, and maintenance is on AI solutions. For example, at Medic, our Community Health Workers, had older Android phones with roughly 2-4GB of RAM on them, and were mainly without network access. Building the application to be reliant on larger models that were unable to run on their phone was effectively a non-starter. I spent time architecting AI solutions that could run on their phones, which we labeled as Runtime AI in our Medic AI strategy that I co-authored with our CEO. This included fine-tuning offline local language voice models to fill out CHT forms.
+4. *What's Smart Task Prioritization?* — I designed and architected the AI smart task prioritization for CHT. This was architected in a manner that once a week, if network access was available, the client would synchronize tasks with a deeper reasoning and higher context model, and on the local offline device it ran a small ML on an existing priority score configuration. I lead a community squad who is building out the feature currently.
+5. *Did you win the AWS Imagine award?* — I was the author, creator of that project that we pitched and built for that AWS Imagine Grant award. Yes, Medic won it. I designed and architected the AI smart task prioritization for CHT.
+6. *What's Buildtime AI?* — A different focus around AI in our strategy was Buildtime AI, and my focus was to pull engineering dreams off the shelf, that were once unattainable or too time consuming, and detail out the implementation path towards them. For Medic, this meant allowing non-technical users the ability to participate in the creation of new features and maintainability of the software. I architected and built the cht-agent, a multi-agent you can deploy, install with all the considerations an SRE brings: cost-effectiveness, model agnostic, locally-owned, context management, curated domain knowledge-corpus.
+7. *What kind of team are you looking for?* — I'm a self-starter, who can identify opportunity, markets, users and create the viable cost-effective fit that works. I co-authored the Medic AI strategy that earned us funding cycles and grants throughout 2025 and 2026. I worked hard cross-dimensionally to effectively identify when AI could be valuable for our goals. I'm looking to join a hyper-speed team, where everyone is like that. Imagine what we could do together, if there was rooms of us.
